.\" Checked against aether/aetherd.nim and aether/wm/daemon_*.nim.
.TH AETHERD 1 "November 22, 2026" CaelWM "CaelWM Manual"
.SH NAME
aetherd \- foreground Aether observation and policy daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aetherd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aetherd
observes Aureole through X11, maintains a local snapshot cache for
.BR aether (1),
publishes filesystem state, reconciles group records, applies built-in
application rules, and notifies session panels.
It remains in the foreground and writes observations to standard output
and diagnostics to standard error. It does not fork, detach, create a PID
file, or launch the window manager.
.SH OPTIONS
There are no supported command-line options or subcommands.
The current entrypoint does not parse command-line arguments, so even
\-\-help and \-\-version do not request informational output.
.SH SOCKET
The Unix-domain stream socket is
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/zephyr/zephyrd.sock
when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is nonempty, otherwise
.BR $WME/zephyrd.sock .
The legacy zephyr names are retained for compatibility and are not replaced
with aether names. With neither variable set, socket creation cannot succeed.
.PP
The daemon creates a missing parent directory, rejects a parent writable
by group or others, and sets socket permissions to owner read/write (0600).
If the path already exists, it attempts a connection: a successful connection
causes startup to fail because another daemon is serving the endpoint.
If the connection fails, it removes the stale path and attempts to bind.
Old and new controller daemons must not serve the same endpoint concurrently.
.PP
Ordinary socket-open failures log IPC_SOCKET_FAILED and retry every second.
A successful open logs IPC_SOCKET_READY followed by the socket path.
This reports socket availability, not completion of the WM baseline.
The socket carries framed, versioned binary requests; use aether for public
queries and readiness synchronization.
.SH LIFECYCLE
After opening IPC, the daemon attaches X11 observation, subscribes to events,
reconciles stale state, and queries the WM snapshot.
It logs a BASELINE and marks readiness after the first successful refresh
has established the baseline and completed initial refresh processing.
.B aether daemon await
waits for that readiness response without an overall timeout.
.PP
Existing baseline clients do not receive ClientAdded rules simply because
the daemon started. Later added clients and selected title changes can
trigger built-in application rules. Rule actions may change window geometry,
groups, focus or stacking according to their compiled policy.
.PP
X11 invalidation events trigger refreshes. Periodic authoritative snapshots
also detect a missing or replacement WM. On observation loss the daemon
invalidates the cache, removes the published WM snapshot, and retries
attachment after about one second while continuing to service local IPC.
Unavailable snapshot queries report errors rather than cached success.
.PP
There is no stop, restart, reload or daemonize command, and no custom
termination handler in the entrypoint. Terminate the foreground process
through the session supervisor or normal process signals. Socket cleanup
on termination is not guaranteed; the next startup probes an existing path.
.SH STATE AND FILES
.TP
.B $WME/wm/snapshot
Normalized WM snapshot, published by temporary-file rename.
Publication requires an absolute WME path.
.TP
.B $GROUP
Group membership and selection mirror, with $GROUP:focus and
$GROUP:deactivated companion trees. Snapshot reconciliation owns group
membership and focus records.
.TP
.B $WINFO/WINID
Geometry history and identity state. DestroyNotify and startup/reconnection
scans schedule current X11 existence checks. Confirmed missing windows lose
state; hidden, unmapped and inactive-group windows retain it. Uncertain
X11 errors preserve state for retry. Active restore-history transactions
defer WINFO cleanup.
.TP
.B $HIDDEN/WINID
Hidden metadata. MapNotify, DestroyNotify and startup/reconnection scans
check current visibility. Visible or destroyed windows lose hidden metadata;
genuinely unmapped windows retain it. This cleanup proceeds independently
of deferred WINFO cleanup, including entries without WINFO records.
.TP
.B $FIFO/desktop, $FIFO/system
Panel notification FIFOs. Relevant snapshot differences send X followed
by a newline to desktop, or S followed by an epoch timestamp and newline
to system. Opens are nonblocking; absent readers do not require panel startup.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B DISPLAY
and
.B XAUTHORITY
select and authorize the X11 connection.
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, WME, GROUP, WINFO, HIDDEN
and
.B FIFO
select the paths above. Rules also use the WMSE screen settings and
CENTER_BIAS described in
.BR aether (1).
The entrypoint does not read a separate daemon configuration file.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Observation and rule events go to standard output. Socket, snapshot,
reconciliation and deferred-cleanup diagnostics go to standard error.
Some messages retain the legacy name zephyrd.
The daemon normally runs indefinitely; fatal startup errors exit nonzero.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aether (1),
.BR aureole (1),
.BR borealis (1)
